\import{fcap-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}

\tag{fcap}
\tag{spin}
\tag{clifford}

\taxon{Theorem}
\title{Witt extension and the regular-subspace interface}

\p{Let #{K} be a field of characteristic different from two, and let
#{(V,Q)} and #{(V',Q')} be isometric finite-dimensional nondegenerate
quadratic spaces. If #{U\subseteq V} is any subspace and #{f:U\to V'} is an
injective linear map satisfying

##{Q'(f(u))=Q(u),}

then #{f} extends to an isometry
#{\widetilde f:(V,Q)\simeq(V',Q')}. For two subspaces of one quadratic space,
the assumption that the ambient spaces are isometric is automatic.}

\p{\citet{Section 4, no. 3, Theorem 1, pp. 71--72}{bourbaki2007algebra}
states the theorem with an arbitrary source subspace. In
\citet{Chapter IV, Section 1.5, Theorem 3, pp. 31--32}{serre1973course}, the
proof explains why degeneracy of that subspace is harmless when the ambient
form is nondegenerate: a radical vector can be paired with a new vector,
enlarging the subspace toward a regular one. The regular case then splits
orthogonally and closes by induction.}

\p{TauCeti proves a complementary form. For a finite-dimensional quadratic
space over a field with #{2} invertible, an isometry equivalence between
subspaces #{W,W'} extends when #{Q|_W} is nondegenerate; the ambient form
itself may be degenerate
\leanref/tauceti{QuadraticMap.IsometryEquiv.exists_extension}. It also chooses
an extension and records its value on #{W}
\leanref/tauceti{QuadraticMap.IsometryEquiv.extension}
\leanref/tauceti{QuadraticMap.IsometryEquiv.extension_apply}. These formal
declarations do not prove the arbitrary-subspace statement above.}

\p{The distinction is visible inside #{Q_{1,3}}. The block

##{A_0=\begin{pmatrix}5/4&3/4\\3/4&5/4\end{pmatrix}}

on the first positive and first negative coordinates, together with the
identity on the other two coordinates, defines an ambient isometry #{A}. It
satisfies #{A(e_0+e_1)=2(e_0+e_1)}, so its restriction to the null line is an
isometry of a degenerate subspace. The classical theorem covers such a line;
the regular-subspace formal declaration does not apply because the source
restriction is degenerate.}
